// Techno Thriller: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== techno-thriller.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Rats
genre: techno-thriller + comedy
tone: Brass-and-mahogany, crackling and impish
era: 1903 | period
setting: A London lecture theatre and a music hall roof nearby | real | city
logline: Minutes before a great inventor demonstrates that his wireless telegraph is perfectly secure, the receiving apparatus on the lecture-hall bench begins tapping out an insult by itself, and his assistant has to find out who is sending it before the audience notices.
premise: The inventor is three hundred miles away on a cliff, about to transmit to London. His company's shares depend on one claim: that a tuned message can be read by no one but its intended receiver and interfered with by nobody. In the lecture theatre a distinguished physicist is to read the message aloud. His assistant {blok} is warming up the receiver when the arc lamp in the lantern begins to flicker in a rhythm he recognises.
synopsis: The first act is ten minutes of sweat. The lamp is ticking Morse. Then the printer starts: one word, over and over, "rats". Then a limerick about a young fellow from abroad who diddled the public. {blok} must keep the lecturer {hartwell} talking, keep the audience from hearing a clattering Morse inker, and find the source, which from its strength must be within a few streets. The real message arrives from the cliff on time, sandwiched in abuse, and is read out to applause. Nobody in the audience knows. The second act is the hunt, and the film shifts into a detective comedy of early electricity. A letter to the newspaper from the outraged professor denounces "scientific hooliganism" and asks who did it. Four days later the culprit answers, in print, under his own name. He is {marchmont}, a stage magician from a family of magicians, inventor of a coin-operated lock and a levitation, who has used wireless in his mind-reading act for years and was hired by the cable telegraph companies, who have a great deal to lose, to find out whether the inventor's claims were true. He had put a mast on the roof of the music hall next door. {blok}, sent to confront him, finds a workshop of trick cabinets and spark coils and a man who says, reasonably: if I can do this with a ten-foot pole, what could a navy do? The third act is the argument, played before the public in letters, lectures and one rematch. The company's man {the_manager} wants a writ. The inventor himself never appears in the film; he is a signal. {blok}, who built half the apparatus and knows precisely how leaky it is, has to choose between a salary and an obvious truth. In the rematch, a staged test, he lets the magician's signal through on purpose and reads it out himself. He is dismissed that evening and engaged the next morning, at the music hall, where the pay is worse and the questions are better. The closing card notes that the word for what the magician did had not been invented yet.
question: Who does a security claim serve: the people relying on it, or the people selling it?
themes: the first hack; showmanship on both sides; honesty about what leaks
audience: General; adults and teenagers who like clever history
viewpoint: With {blok}, at the bench
stakes: A company's fortune, a great man's reputation, and whether the public is told the truth about a technology it is about to depend on
conflict: An assistant paid to defend a claim against a magician paid to break it, and against his own knowledge of which of them is right
ending: The assistant confirms the flaw in public and is sacked. The magician hires him. The inventor's company improves its tuning, without acknowledgement.
experience: A racing first ten minutes, sparks and stage tricks, and the pleasure of a pompous claim punctured
look: Oak benches, brass instruments, an arc lantern; a music hall roof with a mast among chimney pots; paper tape curling on the floor
sound: Morse as percussion, an inker's clatter, an audience's cough. A spark gap like a pistol.
m: expose | institution | loyalty-vs-truth | ironic
@ blok | Arthur Block | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 27 | To get through the demonstration, and then to know | He built the receiver and knows its weaknesses | His salary depends on not saying so | Deft, anxious, honest against his interest | From company man to the magician's engineer | Quick, technical, apologetic | Shirtsleeves, a pair of pliers, acid-burned fingers
@ marchmont | Nevil Marchmont | co-lead | rival | human | he/him | 39 | To show the claim is false, with style | He was hired to, and he resents the inventor's patents | He is a conjuror and nobody trusts a conjuror | Witty, exact, theatrical | From anonymous hooligan to the one who told the truth | Stage patter, perfectly timed | Evening dress, a monocle, a spark coil in a hatbox
@ hartwell | Professor Hartwell | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 53 | A dignified lecture | He has lent his name to the claim | Something on the bench is being rude | Deaf, eminent, irascible | From outrage in print to a private grudging note | Lecture-hall boom | Frock coat, an ear trumpet
@ the_manager | Mr. Cuthbert Hallam | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 45 | The share price | A navy contract is pending | The assistant has a conscience | Smooth, impatient | From soothing to dismissing | Boardroom murmurs | Silk hat, a share certificate
~ blok - marchmont | defender and attacker | he is sent to confront him | he is hired by him
~ blok - hartwell | assistant and lecturer | he keeps him talking | he reads the intruding signal aloud to him
~ blok - the_manager | employee and employer | a rise promised | dismissal at six, with a reference withheld
